    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Appraisal is a clause in most Texas homeowners and commercial property policies that lets either you or the insurer demand an outside valuation when you disagree about the amount of loss. Each side picks an appraiser, the two appraisers pick an umpire, and an award signed by any two of the three sets the amount. You pay your appraiser, the insurer pays its appraiser, and you split the umpire.</p>
<p>It settles <em>how much</em>, not <em>whether</em>. If the carrier says the damage isn't covered, appraisal is the wrong tool. If the carrier agrees hail damaged your roof but pays for six squares when your roofer says thirty, appraisal is often the right one.</p>

<h2>How the appraisal clause works</h2>
<p>Wording varies by policy, so read your own clause. Most follow the same pattern.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Written demand.</strong> Either party sends a written demand for appraisal, usually naming the claim and the disputed amount.</li>
<li><strong>Each side names an appraiser.</strong> The policy typically gives a short window, often counted in days from the demand, to name a competent and impartial appraiser.</li>
<li><strong>The appraisers choose an umpire.</strong> If they can't agree within the time the policy allows, most clauses let either side ask a court to appoint one.</li>
<li><strong>The appraisers inspect and value the loss.</strong> Each appraiser looks at the property and prepares a valuation, usually as a line-item estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Differences go to the umpire.</strong> Where the appraisers agree, that's the number. Where they don't, they submit their differences to the umpire.</li>
<li><strong>The award.</strong> A written award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss. The carrier then applies your deductible, prior payments and any depreciation terms to calculate what it owes.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When to invoke appraisal</h2>
<p>Appraisal works best after the ordinary steps have narrowed the dispute to price and scope. Before you demand it, you should have:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>The carrier's estimate and your own line-item estimate, compared line by line.</li>
<li>A re-inspection request answered, with the carrier's reasons in writing.</li>
<li>A dispute that is about quantities, prices, repair versus replacement, or missing line items, not about whether the loss is covered.</li>
<li>Your policy's deadlines in view, including any time limit on demanding appraisal and the "Suit against us" condition.</li>
</ul>
<p>Appraisal is a poor fit when the carrier denied the claim outright, when the real issue is an exclusion or endorsement, or when the gap is small enough that appraisal fees would eat most of it. For a claim that is still at the re-inspection stage, see <a href="/post/underpaid-hail-damage-insurance-claim/">underpaid hail damage claims</a> first.</p>

<h2>Writing the appraisal demand</h2>
<p>The demand is a short letter, but it sets the boundaries of what the appraisers will value, so get it right. Send it to the adjuster in writing, keep proof of delivery, and include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Your name, the property address, the policy number and the claim number.</li>
<li>A statement that you disagree with the insurer on the amount of loss and are invoking the appraisal provision of the policy.</li>
<li>The name and contact details of your appraiser, if you have already chosen one.</li>
<li>A clear description of what is in dispute, for example the roof, gutters and interior ceilings from the storm on a stated date.</li>
</ul>
<p>Be careful about scope. If you leave out the interior damage or the detached garage, the carrier may argue those items weren't submitted to appraisal. If you include items the carrier denied for coverage reasons, expect a dispute about whether they belong there at all.</p>
<p>Timing matters too. Demanding appraisal doesn't pause your policy's other deadlines, including the deadline to sue in the "Suit against us" condition. Our article on <a href="/post/how-long-do-you-have-to-file-a-hail-claim-in-texas/">how long you have to file a hail claim in Texas</a> covers the deadlines that commonly apply. Before you start, run the numbers through the <a href="/tools/claim-settlement-calculator/">claim settlement calculator</a> so you know what a likely award would actually pay after your deductible and depreciation.</p>

<h2>Choosing your appraiser</h2>
<p>Your appraiser is the most important decision in the process. Look for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Roof and hail experience.</strong> They should be able to walk a roof, recognize hail bruising and soft metal damage, and defend a slope-by-slope opinion.</li>
<li><strong>Estimating skill.</strong> Most appraisal awards are argued in Xactimate line items. An appraiser who can't build or read one is at a disadvantage.</li>
<li><strong>Impartiality.</strong> Many policies require the appraiser to be competent and impartial or disinterested. Ask how the appraiser charges, and avoid arrangements, such as a stake in the repair work, that the carrier could use to challenge the award.</li>
<li><strong>Availability.</strong> After a major Texas hailstorm, good appraisers are busy. Ask how soon they can inspect.</li>
</ul>
<p>We advise clients on appraisal, prepare the estimate and documentation their appraiser relies on, and help them choose an appraiser who fits the claim.</p>

<h2>The umpire</h2>
<p>The umpire only decides the items the two appraisers can't agree on. A good umpire is experienced, independent of both sides, and available. The appraisers usually trade names until they agree. If one side stalls, the court-appointment option in most clauses keeps the process moving.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>What appraisal costs</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost</th><th>Who usually pays</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Your appraiser</td><td>You</td></tr>
<tr><td>The insurer's appraiser</td><td>The insurer</td></tr>
<tr><td>The umpire</td><td>Split equally between you and the insurer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Engineers, roofers or other experts you bring in</td><td>Whoever hires them</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Appraisers set their own fees, and arrangements vary. Ask for the fee terms in writing before you sign anything, and weigh them against the size of the dispute.</p>

<h2>Is the appraisal award binding?</h2>
<p>Generally, yes, on the amount of loss. That cuts both ways: if the award comes in lower than you hoped, you are usually bound by it too. Courts set aside appraisal awards only in narrow circumstances, and you should talk to an attorney if you think one applies.</p>
<p>The award is not the check. The carrier applies the policy to the award: your deductible, what it has already paid, and depreciation terms on ACV or replacement-cost coverage. Ask how those will be applied before the award is signed, so the final payment doesn't surprise you.</p>

<h2>What appraisal can't decide</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div><h3>Appraisal decides</h3><ul><li>How many squares, slopes or components were damaged</li><li>Repair versus replacement, where that is a question of scope</li><li>Unit prices, quantities and missing line items</li><li>The dollar amount of the loss</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>Appraisal does not decide</h3><ul><li>Whether the loss is covered at all</li><li>Whether an exclusion or endorsement applies</li><li>Whether the carrier handled the claim in bad faith</li><li>Whether you met the policy's deadlines and conditions</li></ul></div>
</div>
<p>Some disputes sit on the line between the two, such as whether particular damage was caused by the storm or by age. If that question drives your claim, get legal advice before you demand appraisal. If the carrier denied coverage, read <a href="/post/denied-hail-damage-insurance-claim/">what to do about a denied hail claim</a>.</p>

<h2>TWIA claims have their own appraisal process</h2>
<p>Coastal homeowners insured by the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association follow a different set of rules. TWIA claims are governed by Chapter 2210 of the Texas Insurance Code, and <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.2210.htm" rel="noopener" target="_blank">§2210.574</a> sets out an appraisal process for disputes over the amount of loss on claims TWIA has accepted in whole or in part. It has its own procedure and deadlines, which are separate from those in private policies.</p>
<p>TWIA claims must be filed within one year of the date of damage, and TWIA generally has 60 days to accept or deny after receiving the claim and the information it requested (§2210.205, §2210.573). Read TWIA's acceptance or partial-acceptance notice carefully: it explains the dispute options and the time limits that apply. A coverage denial follows a different path from an appraisal of the amount. Our <a href="/services/hurricane-windstorm-claims/">hurricane and windstorm claims</a> page covers TWIA claims more broadly.</p>

<h2>What to have ready</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>The full policy and the appraisal clause</li>
<li>All carrier estimates, letters and engineer reports</li>
<li>Your line-item estimate with photos keyed to each disputed item</li>
<li>Storm date evidence for your address</li>
<li>Measurement reports, shingle identification and any brittleness test results</li>
<li>A one-page summary of the disputed items and the dollar difference on each</li>
</ul>
